WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:13.310
<v Ira Glass>Parents-- here's all the evidence that you need that TV is bad for kids. Especially public TV. When Sean was 14, he loved watching those British TV shows that are always running on PBS. Masterpiece Theatre, Doctor Who.
<v Ira Glass>父母们——这就是你们所需要的，证明电视对孩子有害的全部证据。尤其是公共电视。肖恩14岁时，非常喜欢看那些在公共电视网（PBS）上常年播出的英剧。比如《杰作剧场》、《神秘博士》。

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:38.920
<v Sean Cole>And then there was this show that I would stay up really late and watch, and tape, and watch over and over again the tapes, called Dempsey & Makepeace. Which was about an American detective who went to London because he had been set up at home. And he was teamed up with a woman, who was this aristocrat named Lady Harriet Makepeace. And I was really on her side, I thought, she's got it going on.
<v Sean Cole>还有一部剧，我会熬夜看，录下来，然后一遍又一遍地看录像带，叫《登普西和梅克皮斯》。讲的是一个美国侦探因为在家乡被陷害而去了伦敦。他与一位名叫哈里特·梅克皮斯夫人的贵族女性搭档。我当时真的很支持她，我觉得，她真是太厉害了。

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.440
<v Ira Glass>You looked down on the American.
<v Ira Glass>你瞧不起那个美国人。

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.980
<v Sean Cole>Oh yeah.
<v Sean Cole>哦，是的。

00:00:42.980 --> 00:01:04.900
<v Ira Glass>What Sean liked about Lady Harriet Makepeace and all the other Brits on TV was their aloofness. How they seemed above it all, how they looked down on Americans, which Sean did also. Convinced there must be something wrong with the nation that produced jocks and bullies who harassed him at school. And sometimes, joking around with his friends, he would talk with a British accent.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩喜欢哈里特·梅克皮斯夫人和电视上其他英国人的地方，就是他们的冷漠。他们看起来高高在上，看不起美国人，肖恩也一样。他确信那个制造了在学校里欺负、骚扰他的运动员和恶霸的国家肯定出了什么问题。有时，在和朋友开玩笑时，他会用英国口音说话。

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:21.460
<v Sean Cole>And then it was just something that spiraled out of control. I know that eventually, I was just using an English accent literally from waking to sleeping. Morning, noon, and night.
<v Sean Cole>然后，这就变得一发不可收拾了。我知道最终，我从起床到睡觉真的都在用英国口音。从早到晚，一直如此。

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:32.320
<v Ira Glass>Sean spoke with a British accent from the time he was 14 until he was 16. And at some point, his mother took him to see a psychiatrist.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩从14岁到16岁一直说着英国口音。有一次，他母亲带他去看心理医生。

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:57.430
<v Sean Cole>He was just really, he must-- I don't know the different schools of psychology, but he was really very confrontive. And he was like, well, you've got to stop doing this, he said, because you're not British. And my mom just sort of sat over next to me, and she sort of went, yeah, to agree with him. And to sort of help him in showing me this.
<v Sean Cole>他真的很，他必须——我不了解不同的心理学流派，但他确实非常爱争辩。他说，你得停止这样做，因为你不是英国人。我妈妈就坐在我旁边，她有点像，是的，同意他的观点。并试图帮他向我证明这一点。

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:15.960
<v Ira Glass>Sean was furious. He had an impulse to lecture the guy on how, in fact, he was British. And the only problem with that was, A, he knew very well that he was not, and B, his mother was sitting right there. She was sure to contradict him. It all just seemed impossible.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩非常愤怒。他有一种冲动，想教训那个家伙，告诉他其实自己就是英国人。唯一的问题是，第一，他很清楚自己不是，第二，他妈妈就坐在那里。她肯定会反驳他。这一切似乎都不可能。

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.640
<v Sean Cole>Because that's what I was thinking. Like, there has to be a way that I could be British still.
<v Sean Cole>因为我当时就是那么想的。就像，一定有办法让我还是英国人。

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.610
<v Ira Glass>There must be a way that this is true, somehow.
<v Ira Glass>一定有办法让这是真的，以某种方式。

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:27.040
<v Sean Cole>Yeah, exactly.
<v Sean Cole>是的，没错。

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:53.650
<v Ira   Glass>Well today on our radio program, stories of people to tell a lie, and they get to the point where they believe the lie more than anyone else does. In other words, stories of people pulling hoaxes on themselves. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira   Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，讲述的是人们撒谎，直到他们自己比任何人都更相信这个谎言的故事。换句话说，关于人们对自己进行欺骗的故事。这里是来自WBEZ芝加哥和国际公共广播电台的《这就是美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:02:53.650 --> 00:03:25.670
<v Ira   Glass>Act one of our program today, The Sun Never Sets On The Moosewood Restaurant, in which two young men, both from small towns, try on new identities, false identities, and what they have to do to keep the lies going. Act two, Conning The Con Men. Nancy Updike reports on a federal sting operation and how it caught con men by setting up a con of its own. Act three, Outperforming the Performers. A girl gets her big break on Broadway by going into a coma. Stay with us.
<v Ira   Glass>今天节目的第一幕，《太阳永不落下——穆斯伍德餐厅》，讲述两个来自小镇的年轻人尝试新身份、假身份，以及他们为了维持谎言所做的一切。第二幕，《欺骗骗子》。南希·厄普代克报道了一次联邦钓鱼执法，以及他们如何通过设下骗局来抓捕骗子。第三幕，《超越表演者》。一个女孩通过装昏迷在百老汇获得了大突破。请继续收听。

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:54.460
<v Ira Glass>Act one, The Sun Never Sets on the Moosewood Restaurant. This is the story of two young people who, for a period in their lives, in their search to figure out who they were, pretended to be people who they were not. We'll hear from Sean Cole and Joel Lovell, starting with Sean. He grew up in a small town in Massachusetts, a town that was approximately 3,350 miles from London.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《太阳永不落下——穆斯伍德餐厅》。这是一个关于两个年轻人的故事，他们在人生的某个阶段，在寻找自我的过程中，假装成了别人。我们将听到肖恩·科尔和乔尔·洛弗尔的故事，从肖恩开始。他在马萨诸塞州的一个小镇长大，那个小镇距离伦敦大约3350英里。

00:03:54.460 --> 00:04:05.330
<v Sean  Cole>It was second nature. It was first nature. To this day I have trouble saying, oh, I faked an accent for two years. I mean, I had an accent for two years.
<v Sean  Cole>这是第二天性。这是第一天性。直到今天，我说“哦，我装了两年口音”都很困难。我的意思是，我确实有两年的口音。

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:17.760
<v Ira Glass>Sean, could I just ask you to take a deep breath and describe for me what you had for lunch today, or perhaps for breakfast this morning, in as close to the accent that you can remember.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩，我能请你深呼吸一下，用你所能记住的最接近那种口音，描述一下你今天午餐吃了什么，或者今天早餐吃了什么吗。

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:34.110
<v Sean Cole>As close I can. OK. I'm going to take a sip of water here. Well, Ira, I had a salad. I had it at the Boston House of Pancakes-- er, Pizza, rather.
<v Sean Cole>尽我所能。好的。我先喝口水。嗯，艾拉，我吃了沙拉。是在波士顿煎饼屋——哦，不，是比萨店吃的。

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.740
<v Ira Glass>What was the beverage?
<v Ira Glass>喝了什么饮料？

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:46.350
<v Sean Cole>It was a Snapple, just lemon-flavored. I don't really like the peach.
<v Sean Cole>是Snapple，柠檬味的。我不太喜欢桃子味的。

00:04:46.350 --> 00:05:03.530
<v Ira  Glass>Joel Lovell's story began when he left the working class town where he grew up, in upstate New York. His parents owned a liquor store in a small town. He was the first member of his family to go to college, and it was an especially big deal because he got into an Ivy League school, Cornell.
<v Ira  Glass>乔尔·洛弗尔的故事始于他离开他长大的工薪阶层小镇，前往纽约州北部。他的父母在小镇上开了一家酒铺。他是家里第一个上大学的人，这尤其了不起，因为他考上了常春藤盟校——康奈尔大学。

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:47.570
<v Joel  Lovell>It was one of those first days of college, you know, when to spend a lot of time-- everybody kind of moves in hordes, and you spend a lot of time in each other's dorm rooms. And there were about, oh, I don't know, 10 or 11 of us in this one guy's room. And we were just like sitting around, eating pizza and talking. And people were talking about where they were from and what their parents did, stuff like that. And there was one guy whose dad was a doctor for the Knicks. There was another guy whose father was an elected representative from New York State. And then this other guy whose father was on the World Court. Literally was a member of the World Court.
<v Joel  Lovell>那是大学开学的第一天，你知道，每个人都聚在一起，你在彼此的宿舍里花了很多时间。那个人的房间里大概有，哦，我不知道，10或11个人。我们只是坐在一起，吃比萨，聊天。大家在聊家乡在哪，父母做什么工作之类的。其中一个人父亲是尼克斯队的医生。另一个人父亲是纽约州的民选代表。还有一个人的父亲在国际法院。真的是国际法院的成员。

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:48.430
<v Ira Glass>Oh, my.
<v Ira Glass>哦，天哪。

00:05:48.430 --> 00:06:01.230
<v Joel Lovell>And so it suddenly seemed like this incredibly impressive group to me. And they seemed sort of worldly in ways that was just beyond my wildest imagination. And worldly beyond what I am now, frankly.
<v Joel Lovell>所以这对我来说突然看起来是一个极其令人印象深刻的群体。他们似乎以某种方式表现得世故，超出了我最疯狂的想象。老实说，甚至比我现在还要世故。

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:58.010
<v Joel Lovell>And I remember sitting there at the time thinking, oh my God, I'm so out of my league here. And then, completely unplanned, I suddenly said, as a slice of pizza was passed to me, this pizza with sausage on top of it. I said, I can't take that because my parents are vegetarians. And then everybody in the room sort of turned and looked at me. Because it wasn't even as if I said well, I'm a vegetarian. But I said, my parents are vegetarians. And there's a sort of puzzled look on everybody in the room. And I said well, and I am too. I've never eaten meat. I'm not entirely-- well, I mean, I have some ideas now about why I said that. But at the time I had no idea what I was saying. It was like suddenly I'd become possessed. And I had to think of something to say about myself that seemed interesting. And vegetarianism was the thing that I chose.
<v Joel Lovell>我记得当时坐在那里想，天哪，我和他们根本不是一个层次的。然后，完全没经过计划，当一块比萨递给我时，上面有香肠，我突然说：我不能吃那个，因为我父母是素食者。然后房间里每个人都转头看着我。因为那甚至不是我说了“我是素食者”。但我说了，“我父母是素食者”。房间里每个人都露出了困惑的表情。我说，嗯，我也是。我从来没吃过肉。我不是完全——好吧，我的意思是，我现在对为什么那么说有一些想法。但当时我根本不知道自己在说什么。就像突然被附体了一样。我必须想出一些关于自己的、看起来很有趣的话题。于是我选择了素食主义。

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:04.130
<v Ira   Glass>Now, did you tell people that you were actually from England?
<v Ira   Glass>那么，你有没有告诉别人你其实来自英国？

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:17.450
<v Sean Cole>No, no. Never that I was from Britain. But in a way, that I was British, you know. There was a real distinction there for me. Like I'd taken it on. Like I was culturally British now.
<v Sean Cole>没有，没有。从来没说过我来自英国。但在某种程度上，我就是英国人，你知道。对我来说，这之间有真正的区别。就像我已经接受了它。就像我在文化上已经是英国人了。

00:07:17.450 --> 00:08:00.710
<v Joel Lovell>Well, I think what it was is, I think I did some sort of calculus that took like an nanosecond in my head. And I thought, I can't actually lie about what my parents do. But I think the connections that I was making were this. That somehow, because I was from this town in the sticks, if my folks were vegetarians, then the whole history that that suggested was that they were sort of these kind of leftist academic radicals who had sort of dropped out of society and gone back to the land. And I was living in this bumpkin town in upstate New York, and my folks were living some sort of life that was driven by their political philosophy, rather than I was just a guy who grew up in upstate New York.
<v Joel Lovell>嗯，我想原因在于，我在脑子里做了一些计算，大概花了一纳秒。我想，关于我父母是做什么的，我不能撒谎。但我当时的联想是这样的：因为我来自那个乡下的偏远小镇，如果我父母是素食者，那么这背后的全部历史就意味着他们是那种左翼学术激进分子，退出了社会，回归了田园。而我当时住在纽约州北部那个土气的镇子上，我父母过的是一种由其政治哲学驱动的生活，而不是我只是一个在纽约州北部成长起来的普通家伙。

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:10.050
<v Sean Cole>I did the old kid thing of wishing that my real British parents would come and tell me I was adopted and take me back to London.
<v Sean Cole>我做了那种老套的孩子的行为，希望我真正的英国父母会出现，告诉我我是被领养的，然后带我回伦敦。

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:29.390
<v Joel Lovell>So I'm sitting there in the room and all these guys are looking at me. And they're like, dude, what do you eat? And suddenly I realized, in that moment, how little I knew about vegetarianism. I kind of tried to be real vague about it. You know, we eat salads and lentils. I remember saying lentils a lot.
<v Joel Lovell>所以我坐在房间里，所有那些家伙都看着我。他们说，老兄，你吃什么？在那一刻，我突然意识到我对素食主义了解得太少了。我试图对此说得很模糊。你知道，我们吃沙拉和小扁豆。我记得我经常说小扁豆。

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:49.680
<v Sean Cole>And there was a gap, certainly, in my education. Because I would be using words that Americans just don't use. I would, instead of saying drug store, I would say chemist. Or I would try my best to remember to say bonnet instead of hood, or boot instead of trunk. But I often couldn't.
<v Sean Cole>而且我的教育肯定有断层。因为我会使用美国人根本不用的词。我会说“药剂师”（chemist）而不是“药店”（drug store）。或者我会尽力记住说“引擎盖”（bonnet）而不是“车前盖”（hood），说“后备箱”（boot）而不是“行李箱”（trunk）。但我经常记不住。

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:55.450
<v Joel Lovell>On the meal plan I ended up eating a lot of big piles of iceberg lettuce. And chickpeas.
<v Joel Lovell>在学校用餐计划里，我最后吃了很多大堆的冰山生菜。还有鹰嘴豆。

00:08:55.450 --> 00:09:05.060
<v Ira  Glass>And during that time, would you find yourself sneaking to go to get meat somewhere?
<v Ira  Glass>那段时间，你会不会发现自己偷偷溜出去买肉吃？

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:53.410
<v Joel Lovell>Yeah, definitely. At first I would go really far from campus in order to have a BLT. There's this diner downtown in Ithaca, and it felt incredibly illicit. I'd be sitting there and I'd have some reading material or something with me, and I'd be the lonely guy in my booth. And I would order the BLT and I would watch it coming from across the room, with its toothpick in the top of it. And a side of French fries with this meat gravy on top. And when it landed on the table it would just seem like this incredibly wonderful moment. When you're doing something just totally unlike what anybody would expect of you.
<v Joel Lovell>会，绝对会。起初，我会走离校园很远的地方去吃BLT三明治。伊萨卡市中心有一家餐馆，那感觉极其非法。我会坐在那里，带点阅读材料什么的，我会是卡座里孤独的家伙。我会点BLT，看着它从房间另一头送过来，上面插着牙签。还有一份配着肉汁的炸薯条。当它落到桌子上时，那简直是一个极其美妙的时刻。当你做的事情完全出乎所有人的意料时。

00:09:53.410 --> 00:10:27.240
<v Sean Cole>I was a nobody. I was living in an extremely small, kind of rural town, in the middle of nowhere. I guess, in a way, this was my way of travelling. And of being somebody. And sort of achieving an identity. Which I guess I didn't feel like I had. I didn't feel like-- I'm just sort of realizing this now-- but I guess I didn't feel as though I had anything that made me up.
<v Sean Cole>我是一个无名小卒。我住在一个极小的、乡村化的、偏僻的小镇上。我想，在某种程度上，这就是我旅行和成为某人的方式。以及获得某种身份认同。我想我当时觉得自己并没有这些。我不觉得——我只是现在才意识到这一点——但我猜我不觉得我有什么能构成我的东西。

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:52.080
<v Joel Lovell>I mean, what I realized fairly quickly is that if this is going to be believable, I actually have to believe in it. But I also began to, not only believe, but really sort of take on as my persona, all the stuff that I imagined was associated with vegetarianism.
<v Joel Lovell>我的意思是，我很快意识到，如果这要让人相信，我必须真的相信它。但我也开始，不仅是相信，而是真正地将我所想象的与素食主义相关的一切，作为我的人格呈现出来。

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.880
<v Ira Glass>Like what?
<v Ira Glass>比如什么？

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:02.020
<v Joel Lovell>Well, certain political convictions, and ways of dress.
<v Joel Lovell>嗯，某些政治信念，还有着装方式。

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:18.480
<v Sean Cole>I wore ripped jeans and I wore combat boots. But I also wore a kind of stage jacket that you would see in a community theater production of Hamlet.
<v Sean Cole>我穿破洞牛仔裤和战斗靴。但我也穿一种舞台夹克，你会在社区剧院制作的《哈姆雷特》中看到的那种。

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:40.480
<v Joel Lovell>Yeah, I bought sandals. I very specifically remember going down to this thrift store in downtown Ithaca and buying a pair of fatigue shorts, which just seemed like I might as well have been Che Guevara at that point. As far I was concerned I was a dangerous leftist.
<v Joel Lovell>是的，我买了凉鞋。我非常清楚地记得去伊萨卡市中心的一家旧货店，买了一件军装短裤，在那一刻，我感觉自己简直就像切·格瓦拉。就我而言，我是一个危险的左翼分子。

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:53.050
<v Ira Glass>Did you, at any point during this, find yourself in the following argument where you would say, I've never had a hamburger, and somebody would insist, oh, you must have had meat some point. And then you had to argue your side.
<v Ira Glass>在你经历这段时间的任何时候，有没有遇到过这样的争论：你说“我从来没吃过汉堡”，而别人坚持说“哦，你肯定在某个时候吃过肉”。然后你不得不为自己辩解。

00:11:53.050 --> 00:12:29.730
<v Sean Cole>Yeah, definitely. It wasn't pretty. And of course, I had grown up-- just to put this in context for a second, if you don't mind. I mean, not only had I had hundreds of hamburgers, and gone to the McDonald's drive through hundreds of times, but the counter point situation that I always think about when I remember this time was that, when I was a senior in high school, my family, for time saving reasons, decided that a great thing to do would be to go to Arby's Roast Beef. I don't know if you have those out in Chicago, I think they're countrywide.
<v Sean Cole>是的，绝对有。那场面可不好看。当然，我是在——为了给这加个背景，如果你不介意的话。我的意思是，我不仅吃过几百个汉堡，去过几百次麦当劳的免下车窗口，而且我总是回想起那个反面例子：当我高三时，为了省时间，家里决定去吃Arby's烤牛肉三明治。我不知道你们芝加哥有没有，我觉得它们遍布全国。

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:59.210
<v Sean Cole>So my dad and I would go to Arby's, on say, a Thursday afternoon or something. Or after I got out of school. And we would go in there and we would buy 48 Arby's roast beef sandwiches. And they would put them in this cardboard box. And we would bring home this giant box full of those tin foil covered Arby's roast beef sandwiches, and we would stuff them in our freezer. We would freeze the Arby's roast beef sandwiches and then we would have them there.
<v Sean Cole>所以我爸和我会在比如周四下午什么的去Arby's。或者放学后。我们会进去买48个Arby's烤牛肉三明治。他们会把它们装在这个纸箱子里。我们会把装满锡纸包裹的Arby's烤牛肉三明治的巨型箱子带回家，然后塞进冷冻室。我们会冷冻那些三明治，然后随时吃。

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.000
<v Ira Glass>Buns and all?
<v Ira Glass>连面包一起？

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:08.520
<v Joel Lovell>Buns and all, yeah. And so we would have them there as ready made snacks whenever we might want one. I mean, that's the kind of meat eating that my family was engaged in.
<v Joel Lovell>连面包一起，是的。所以我们随时想吃就有现成的点心。我的意思是，这就是我家人参与的那种吃肉方式。

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:20.440
<v Sean   Cole>The other things was that I had these run ins with doubting my British identity,
<v Sean   Cole>另一件事是，我会对我的英国身份产生怀疑。

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:21.620
<v Ira Glass>Oh really?
<v Ira Glass>哦，真的吗？

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:54.830
<v Sean Cole>Yeah, as though it were slipping away. And I would really go nuts at that point. And there was one time it happened at home. I was at home, and I was like, oh my god, I have to do something. I have to affirm my devotion. I opened up the window and I psyched myself to do it. I was just like, oh man, if I don't do this, it won't come back. And I opened up the window, and then I screamed-- this is in the middle of the night, or, ten at night. And I screamed I love England, of course in a British accent, outside the window.
<v Sean Cole>是的，好像它正在溜走。在那一刻，我会彻底崩溃。有一次是在家里发生的。我在家里，心想，天哪，我必须做点什么。我必须确认我的忠诚。我打开窗户，给自己打气，准备去做。我就想，天哪，如果我不这样做，它就不会回来了。我打开窗户，然后尖叫——这是在大半夜，或者晚上十点。我冲着窗外尖叫“我爱英国”，当然是用英国口音。

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:58.650
<v Ira Glass>And then you felt better? You felt like you had reasserted yourself?
<v Ira Glass>然后你感觉好些了吗？你觉得你重新确立了自己？

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.720
<v Sean Cole>I felt like I had done something, at least.
<v Sean Cole>我觉得至少我做了一些事情。

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:01.880
<v Ira Glass>For England.
<v Ira Glass>为了英国。

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:06.240
<v Sean Cole>Yeah. I had fortified my Britishness.
<v Sean Cole>是的。我巩固了我的英国属性。

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:21.520
<v Joel   Lovell>I would find myself in these conversations where people were saying, you've never had a McDonald's hamburger. What kind of American, 18 year old American, has never had a hamburger from McDonald's?
<v Joel   Lovell>我经常发现自己陷入这样的对话：人们说“你从来没吃过麦当劳汉堡。一个18岁的美国人怎么可能从来没吃过麦当劳的汉堡？”

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:23.180
<v Ira Glass>Quite a legitimate question, I would add.
<v Ira Glass>这是一个非常合理的问题，我要补充一点。

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:54.970
<v Joel Lovell>Absolutely, absolutely. And I would say, yeah, you know. I've just never had one, they scare me. And I would talk about the ways-- and I would make up these stories about how I'd come close a couple of times. How a friend of mine in high school had bought me a Big Mac and there I was, sitting on the front seat of his car, and I almost ate it, and then couldn't bring myself to do it. So there was all the drama that I lied about.
<v Joel Lovell>当然，当然。我会说，是的，你知道。我只是从来没吃过，它们让我害怕。我会谈论——我会编造一些故事，讲我曾经有几次差点就吃了。讲我高中时的一个朋友给我买了个巨无霸，我就坐在他车的前座上，差点就吃了，结果还是没能下得去口。所以，我撒了谎，制造了所有的戏剧性。

00:14:54.970 --> 00:15:07.090
<v Joel Lovell>My mom and Dad came down to visit for parents' weekend, and they were really proud that I was going there, and really excited to come down. And they came down to visit.
<v Joel Lovell>我的爸妈来参加父母周末，他们为我上这所学校感到非常自豪，非常兴奋地赶来。他们来探望我。

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:12.970
<v Ira Glass>And really proud because you were the first generation to go to college, you made it into this Ivy League school, it was a big, big deal.
<v Ira Glass>而且非常自豪，因为你是家里第一代上大学的人，你考进了这所常春藤名校，这真的是件大事。

00:15:12.970 --> 00:16:01.460
<v Joel Lovell>Right, exactly. Exactly. And so they drove down from Camillus, which is about between an hour and an hour and a half, they came down. And in that week leading up to parents' weekend, everybody's talking about their parents coming, and everybody's making reservations at restaurants, where to eat on Saturday night, and everybody's sort of planning on taking their parents to the football game on Saturday during the day. And it suddenly occurred to me, this real panic set it, that my parents would come down, and we would go to a football game and my dad would buy a hot dog. And somebody across the field would see Mr. Lovell eating a hot dog. And then, of course, the cat would be out of the bag.
<v Joel Lovell>对，没错。没错。所以他们从卡米勒斯开车过来，大概是一个到两个半小时的车程，他们赶来了。在那个父母周末到来前的星期里，每个人都在谈论他们的父母要来，都在预订餐馆，周六晚上去哪吃，每个人都在计划周六白天带父母去看橄榄球比赛。我突然产生了一种真正的恐慌，担心我的父母过来，我们去看球赛，我爸会去买热狗。球场对面有人会看到洛弗尔先生在吃热狗。那么，谎言当然就会穿帮了。

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:23.170
<v Joel Lovell>And so I thought, I've got to make a reservation at a restaurant. At someplace either, A, where nobody else's parents will be, or at a vegetarian restaurant. And so what I did was make a reservation at the Moosewood Restaurant, which is in Ithaca. There's the Moosewood cookbooks that are out.
<v Joel Lovell>所以我心想，我得在餐馆订个位。去一个要么，A，没有别人父母去的地方，或者是一家素食餐馆。所以我做的是在伊萨卡的穆斯伍德餐厅订了位。那边有穆斯伍德的烹饪书出版。

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:24.410
<v Ira Glass>Vegetarian cookbooks.
<v Ira Glass>素食烹饪书。

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:50.170
<v Joel Lovell>Exactly, yeah. And it's this nice little vegetarian restaurant in Ithaca, and a slightly famous place. But then we got there. And I remember sitting down at the table in the Moosewood, and the bowls are these kind of carved wooden bowls, and everything about it feels like a vegetarian restaurant.
<v Joel Lovell>没错，是的。这是伊萨卡一家不错的小型素食餐馆，稍微有点名气。但我们到了那里。我记得坐在穆斯伍德的桌边，碗是那种木雕碗，关于它的一切都感觉像是一家素食餐厅。

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:54.800
<v Ira Glass>And not just a vegetarian restaurant, but kind of a cartoon of a vegetarian restaurant.
<v Ira Glass>而且不仅仅是一家素食餐厅，更像是一种素食餐厅的漫画版本。

00:16:54.800 --> 00:17:25.300
<v Joel Lovell>Exactly. Exactly. And I was looking at my parents across the table, and they were dressed up and they were excited to be coming down, and I could tell my dad was sitting there and sort of perusing the menu and thinking, well, maybe this lentil salad will be good. Or whatever. And I could he was sitting there thinking geeze, I just drove an hour and a half, all I want is a steak, and a baked potato, and a beer. And there I was bringing them here.
<v Joel Lovell>没错。没错。我看着桌子对面的父母，他们盛装打扮，为能来这里感到兴奋，我可以看出我爸坐在那里，仔细浏览菜单，心想，好吧，也许这个小扁豆沙拉会不错。或者别的什么。我可以看出他坐在那儿心想，天哪，我刚开了个把小时的车，我只想吃块牛排，一个烤土豆，喝杯啤酒。而我却带他们来了这里。

00:17:25.300 --> 00:18:08.440
<v Joel Lovell>But they were so game about it. They were so willing to go along with it because, for some reason, they thought I really wanted to bring them there. And I just thought, geeze, you know, these people, my parents, have really given up a lot for me to come there. I mean, financially they were really stretching themselves, and we were taking out all sorts of loans, and all of those things that people do in order to go to college. They never complained once about doing it. They just wanted to come down and see me there and feel all proud that I was there, and I was sort of hiding them out in this vegetarian restaurant. And I felt so bad about it afterwards.
<v Joel Lovell>但他们真的很配合。他们非常愿意顺着我来，因为出于某种原因，他们以为我真的很想带他们来这里。我心想，天哪，你知道，这些人，我的父母，真的为了让我来这里付出了很多。我的意思是，在经济上他们真的很吃力，我们贷了各种款，所有那些人们为了上大学而做的事情。他们从没抱怨过一次。他们只是想来看看我，为我在那里感到自豪，而我却把他们藏在了一家素食餐馆里。事后我感到非常难过。

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:29.100
<v Joel Lovell>And they never once complained. They went home, and I sort of imagine them stopping at a Hardy's just outside of Ithaca, getting a burger as soon as they say goodbye. But after that I just thought geeze, I've got to find some way to come clean about this.
<v Joel Lovell>他们一次也没抱怨过。他们回家了，我甚至想象他们在伊萨卡郊外停在哈迪餐厅，刚道别就买了个汉堡吃。但那之后我心想，天哪，我必须找个办法坦白这件事。

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:40.720
<v Ira  Glass>I mean, is it OK if your child decides to express themself in an alternate personality for a period of two years.
<v Ira  Glass>我的意思是，如果你的孩子决定在两年的时间里以另一种人格来表达自己，这可以吗？

00:18:40.720 --> 00:19:04.730
<v Sean Cole>I think there's-- it's funny, I never thought I would say this-- but I think there's nothing wrong with that. I never thought I would say it because I wish that I hadn't done it now. But maybe I learned something from doing it. I mean, I think that that is par for the course. Now I think that's part of growing up.
<v Sean Cole>我想这里有——这很有趣，我从没想过我会这么说——但我认为这没什么错。我从没想过我会这么说，因为我现在希望我当时没这么做。但也许我在这样做中学到了一些东西。我的意思是，我认为这很正常。现在我认为这是成长的一部分。

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:09.250
<v Joel Lovell>I think it was probably necessary for me at that time in my life.
<v Joel Lovell>我想这对我人生的那个阶段来说可能是必要的。

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:11.410
<v Ira Glass>Because it gave you more confidence.
<v Ira Glass>因为它给了你更多的自信。

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:17.990
<v Joel Lovell>Yeah, and there was some ridge that that this allowed me to cross.
<v Joel Lovell>是的，这让我跨越了某种鸿沟。

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:29.130
<v Ira       Glass>Joel Lovell and Sean Cole. Joel Lovell is a writer in New York City.
<v Ira       Glass>乔尔·洛弗尔和肖恩·科尔。乔尔·洛弗尔是纽约市的一名作家。

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:35.740
<v Sean Cole>Sean Cole works in public radio in Boston.
<v Sean Cole>肖恩·科尔在波士顿从事公共广播工作。

