00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:43.370
<v Ira Glass>Act two, Conning the Con Men. The American legal system, for the most part, does not uphold the principle of eye for an eye. If you steal somebody's car, the police do not steal your car in return. If they catch you selling marijuana, they do not sell marijuana to you as your punishment. But if you're in the business of running scams, authorities catch you by running a scam on you. This is the story of a con man who made millions by fooling people over the phone, until he was the one who got fooled. Nancy Updike reports.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，《欺骗骗子》。美国法律体系在很大程度上并不坚持“以眼还眼”的原则。如果你偷了别人的车，警察不会反过来偷你的车。如果你被抓到卖大麻，他们不会以卖大麻给你作为惩罚。但如果你是做诈骗生意的，当局抓你的方式就是对你进行诈骗。这是一个骗子通过电话愚弄人们赚了数百万美元，直到他自己被愚弄的故事。南希·厄普代克报道。

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:54.140
<v Nancy  Updike>The guy's name is David Diamond. That's his actual name. He was one of the most successful salesman in one of the longest running telemarketing scams in Los Angeles history.
<v Nancy  Updike>这个人的名字叫大卫·戴安德。那是他的真名。他是洛杉矶历史上最持久的电话诈骗案之一中最成功的推销员。

00:20:54.140 --> 00:20:58.830
<v Dale Sekovich>David Diamond was a salesman at a boiler room.
<v Dale Sekovich>大卫·戴安德是某电话中心的一名推销员。

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:05.780
<v Nancy Updike>This is Dale Sekovich. He's been a Federal Trade Commission investigator for 29 years. He's the one who busted Diamond.
<v Nancy Updike>这是戴尔·塞科维奇。他担任联邦贸易委员会调查员已经29年了。正是他抓获了戴安德。

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:21.840
<v Dale Sekovich>He was living in a very expensive home up in the hills of Woodland Hills. He drove a custom Porsche Carrera that he had shipped over here by airplane from Germany from the factory. They lived very high on the hog.
<v Dale Sekovich>他住在伍德兰希尔斯山上一个非常昂贵的家里。他开着一辆定制的保时捷卡雷拉，那是他从德国工厂空运过来的。他们的生活极其奢侈。

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:53.640
<v Nancy Updike>David Diamond was just one of a whole bunch of guys making money hand over fist in an operation in southern California that was basically running the same scam over and over, under different names, for seven years. It was an investment scheme. Give us your money and we'll put it into this great 900 number business, or this online shopping network, or this hot new internet service provider. Needless to say, no one ever made a dime, except the people running the scam who cleared $40 million.
<v Nancy Updike>大卫·戴安德只是在南加州经营的一大批赚取暴利的人之一，该运营七年来基本上是在不同名称下反复进行同样的骗局。这是一个投资计划。给我们钱，我们会把它投入到这个很棒的900电话业务，或者是这个在线购物网络，或者这个热门的新互联网服务提供商中。不用说，除了运营骗局的人捞到了4000万美元之外，从来没有人赚过一分钱。

00:21:53.640 --> 00:22:33.590
<v Nancy Updike>Since Diamond was one of the operation's top salesmen, he made $2 million in commissions in just four years on the job. He got 30% of whatever he talked a person into investing. That means he personally conned people out of more than $6 million. The FTC caught Diamond and the others in the operation essentially by conning the con men. They had volunteers pose as dupes and record their phone calls. Because the FTC brought a case against the operation Diamond worked in, some of those recordings are now part of the public record. I got Dale Sekovich to listen to the tapes with me, and talk about David Diamond and the FBI volunteer who caught him.
<v Nancy Updike>由于戴安德是该运营中最顶尖的推销员之一，他仅仅用了四年时间就在工作中获得了200万美元的佣金。他拿走了他劝说人们投资金额的30%。这意味着他个人从人们手中诈骗了超过600万美元。联邦贸易委员会抓获戴安德和运营中的其他人，本质上是通过欺骗这些骗子。他们让志愿者伪装成受骗者并录下他们的通话。由于联邦贸易委员会对戴安德工作的运营提起了诉讼，其中一些录音现在已成为公共记录的一部分。我让戴尔·塞科维奇和我一起听这些录音，并谈谈大卫·戴安德以及抓获他的联邦调查局志愿者。

00:22:33.590 --> 00:23:05.180
<v Dale Sekovich>The woman on the tape, I can't tell you her real name, but she uses the alias of Marge. She assumed the identity of a person who is named Marge. Marge was a real person who we in law enforcement and who people in the telemarketing business refer to as a mooch. A mooch is someone who will essentially buy anything from anybody who calls her on the telephone. In fact she did, over a number of years. She spent hundreds of thousands of dollars--
<v Dale Sekovich>录音里的那个女人，我不能告诉你她的真名，但她用了马吉这个化名。她假设了马吉这个人的身份。马吉是一个真实的人，我们执法人员和电话营销行业的人称之为“冤大头”。冤大头是指那些本质上会从任何打电话给她的人那里买任何东西的人。事实上，她确实在几年内这么做了。她花费了数十万美元——

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:06.170
<v Nancy Updike>The real Marge.
<v Nancy Updike>真正的马吉。

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:56.700
<v Dale Sekovich>The real Marge spent hundreds of thousands of dollars on bogus prize promotions, investments, gold coins, you name it. So the FBI went to Marge and said, we really think that we need to take your telephone number away from you, because it's being used to ruin your life. So once Marge agreed to that, her telephone number was installed in the home of an FBI volunteer. And that volunteer, every time that phone line rang, the Marge line, that volunteer would pick up that telephone, and answer it, and pose as Marge.
<v Dale Sekovich>真正的马吉花了数十万美元在虚假的奖品促销、投资、金币等等上面。所以联邦调查局找到马吉说，我们真的认为我们需要没收你的电话号码，因为它正在被用来毁掉你的生活。一旦马吉同意了这一点，她的电话号码就被安装在了一名联邦调查局志愿者家里。而那名志愿者，每次那条电话线响起，那条马吉专线，志愿者就会拿起电话，接听，并伪装成马吉。

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.070
<v David Diamond>Marge?
<v David Diamond>马吉？

00:23:58.070 --> 00:23:58.570
<v Marge>Yes?
<v Marge>是的？

00:23:58.570 --> 00:24:00.380
<v David Diamond>It's David Diamond, how are you?
<v David Diamond>我是大卫·戴安德，你好吗？

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:01.600
<v Marge>Oh, I'm OK. It's kind of warm here.
<v Marge>哦，我还好。这里天气有点暖和。

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:06.530
<v David Diamond>Yeah. I set you up a video and a package.
<v David Diamond>是的。我给你准备了一个视频和包裹。

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:07.640
<v Marge>Yes, I have it.
<v Marge>是的，我收到了。

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:25.980
<v David Diamond>OK. The video is with regard to Mark Erickson. Mark Erickson is the person who is heading up the program. And he is very successful in taking upstart companies and making them successful. You've probably heard of Hard Copy. He's the original producer of Hard Copy.
<v David Diamond>好的。视频是关于马克·埃里克森的。马克·埃里克森是负责该项目的人。他在让初创公司取得成功方面非常成功。你可能听说过《硬拷贝》节目。他是《硬拷贝》的原制片人。

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:27.190
<v Marge>I've heard of it, yes.
<v Marge>我听说过，是的。

00:24:27.190 --> 00:24:28.060
<v David Diamond>OK.
<v David Diamond>好的。

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:31.810
<v Nancy Updike>You're smiling as you listen to this. What are you smiling about?
<v Nancy Updike>你听这些的时候在笑。你在笑什么？

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:54.350
<v Dale Sekovich>Well I'm smiling because it's been a while since I've heard Marge, and she sounds so old, and so fragile, and such an easy mark, when in fact she's this sharp FBI informant. She doesn't look as old as she sounds, trust me. So that's one aspect of it. The other aspect is this whole Mark Erickson thing.
<v Dale Sekovich>嗯，我在笑，因为我已经很久没听到马吉的声音了，她听起来那么苍老，那么脆弱，是一个多么容易上钩的受害者，而事实上她却是这个精明的联邦调查局线人。相信我，她看起来没听起来那么老。所以这是其中的一个方面。另一个方面是这个马克·埃里克森的事情。

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:56.040
<v Nancy Updike>Yeah, is he a real person?
<v Nancy Updike>是的，他是真人吗？

