<v Dale Sekovich>Exactly. They want to make this something people can relate to.
<v Dale Sekovich>完全正确。他们想让这成为人们能产生共鸣的东西。

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:53.180
<v David Diamond>Here's the thing. You have to invest everything you've got, or do nothing at all. And I'll say that again. You should invest everything you have. You should transfer all of your investments into this program or do nothing. It doesn't make sense to do just a little bit. You should think about doing a million dollars in this program.
<v David Diamond>这就是重点。你要么投资你所有的一切，要么什么都别做。我会再说一遍。你应该投资你所拥有的一切。你应该把你所有的投资转入这个项目，或者什么都别做。只投入一点点没意义。你应该考虑在这个项目中投入一百万美元。

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:59.010
<v Marge>Oh. I don't know. That's a lot of money.
<v Marge>哦。我不知道。那是很多钱。

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:12.100
<v David Diamond>You need to liquidate every nickel you've got. You either want to be in this situation, you either want to be in the situation wholeheartedly and upgrade your investments, or you don't. My suggestion to you is just do the whole thing.
<v David Diamond>你需要清算你所拥有的每一分钱。你要么想处于这种情况，要么全心全意地加入并升级你的投资，要么就别做。我对你的建议是做全部。

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:15.460
<v Marge>Well, I would never liquidate everything I have.
<v Marge>嗯，我永远不会清算我拥有的一切。

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:18.860
<v David  Diamond>My question is, why not?
<v David  Diamond>我的问题是，为什么不？

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:24.760
<v Marge>Because there's always gambles in anything like this.
<v Marge>因为任何像这样的事情总是有赌博成分。

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:26.950
<v David Diamond>Anything like what?
<v David Diamond>像什么样的？

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:28.850
<v Marge>Well, any investment like this.
<v Marge>嗯，任何像这样的投资。

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:30.930
<v David Diamond>Like this? What does that mean?
<v David Diamond>像这样的？那是什么意思？

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:34.610
<v Marge>Well, anything you invest in, there's always a gamble.
<v Marge>嗯，你投资的任何东西，总是有赌博。

00:26:34.610 --> 00:27:02.360
<v Nancy Updike>Now I want to ask you, you told me once that you thought he sounded nervous on this tape. And in this part where she's saying, you know, an investment like this. And he's sort of questioning her, well, what do you mean like this. I wonder, do you have any sense that he's suspicious that she might know what he's up to. I mean, did they know that volunteers are out there trying to trap them posing as dupes?
<v Nancy Updike>现在我想问你，你曾经告诉我，你觉得他在录音中听起来很紧张。在她说“像这样的投资”的这一部分，他质问她“你说的像这样是什么意思”。我想知道，你有没有感觉到他怀疑她可能知道他在搞什么鬼？我的意思是，他们知道外面有志愿者试图通过伪装成受骗者来引诱他们吗？

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:47.850
<v David Diamond>No. Since we talked about this tape last, I actually had a revelation that came to me as to why. I listened to over 40 individual tapes of David Diamond. Of conversations with Marge and conversations with others over the course of about a year. And one of the things, when you've listen to all of them, you find that David, in the earlier part of that year, was much more sweet and cautious, and trying to bond with these women. And patient. And sometimes would spend an hour on the phone with them. The tape would be an hour long.
<v David Diamond>不知道。自从我们上次谈论这段录音以来，我实际上有了一个关于为什么的启示。我听了超过40段大卫·戴安德的录音。在这一年左右的时间里，与马吉的对话和其他人的对话。当你听完所有这些之后，你会发现大卫在这一年的早期，要甜言蜜语和谨慎得多，并试图与这些女性建立联系。很有耐心。有时会在电话上和她们聊一个小时。录音会长达一小时。

00:27:47.850 --> 00:28:16.350
<v David Diamond>But this tape was made towards the very end of that year period. Probably within a week or two of our raid. Having gone in on the raid, and searched David Diamond's desk that day, I came to realize that David Diamond was starting to question whether he wanted to do this anymore. He was starting to really have some concerns about--
<v David Diamond>但这段录音是在那一年接近尾声时录制的。可能在我们突袭前一两周。在突袭行动中进去并搜查了大卫·戴安德的办公桌那天，我开始意识到大卫·戴安德开始质疑他是否还想再做这件事了。他开始真正对——产生了一些担忧。

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:18.900
<v Nancy  Updike>Moral concerns?
<v Nancy  Updike>道德上的担忧？

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:45.980
<v Dale Sekovich>Moral concerns about what they were doing. And I believe that in these last couple of weeks, and it's kind of shown in this tape, he was becoming a little bit desperate. He wanted to make a couple of more big hits, and he just couldn't figure out why this woman wasn't going to write him a check. So he started getting frustrated. And it comes out in his voice.
<v Dale Sekovich>对他们所做事情的道德担忧。我相信在最后这几周，这段录音也表现了出来，他变得有点绝望了。他想再捞几笔大的，但他就是搞不懂为什么这个女人不给他写支票。所以他开始变得沮丧。这在他的声音里流露出来了。

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:52.430
<v Nancy  Updike>What evidence did you see that he was starting to have moral qualms about what he was doing?
<v Nancy  Updike>你看到了什么证据表明他开始对他所做的事情产生道德顾虑？

00:28:52.430 --> 00:29:02.190
<v Dale  Sekovich>David had become a born again. There were religious tracts all over his office, and posters on his wall.
<v Dale  Sekovich>大卫成了重生派教徒。他的办公室里到处都是宗教小册子，墙上贴着海报。

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:03.630
<v Nancy Updike>Just recently?
<v Nancy Updike>是最近吗？

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:29.510
<v Dale Sekovich>I don't know exactly what the time frame was. We do know that he had given a lot of the money that he had made to his church. And we believe that a lot of that was sort of a self-imposed penance, that he can justify what he was doing because he was giving his tithings. This money that he was taking from these poor victims into his church.
<v Dale Sekovich>我不知道具体的时间框架。我们确实知道他把自己赚来的很多钱捐给了他的教堂。我们认为这在某种程度上是一种自我强加的忏悔，他可以通过因为他进行了什一奉献来为自己的行为辩护。他从这些可怜的受害者那里拿来的钱捐给了他的教堂。

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:45.430
<v David  Diamond>If $30,000 is what you made on every $5,000, and you put $50,000 into this program, that's $300,000 return. That's why I'm telling you, you need to do a million dollars in this program.
<v David  Diamond>如果你每5000美元能赚到3万美元，而你投入5万美元到这个项目中，那就是30万美元的回报。这就是为什么我告诉你，你需要在这个项目中投入一百万美元。

00:29:45.430 --> 00:29:56.770
<v Marge>I don't know. I would never put that much in, in any program.
<v Marge>我不知道。我永远不会在任何项目中投入那么多钱。

00:29:56.770 --> 00:30:02.760
<v David  Diamond>Do you have an obligation to yourself as an investor to make the most amount of money possible?
<v David  Diamond>作为一个投资者，你是否有义务去赚取尽可能多的钱？

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:12.030
<v Marge>Well, you know, at my age it's really not that-- what do I want to say-- I have enough to live on for the rest of my life.
<v Marge>嗯，你知道，在我的年龄，这真的不是——我该怎么说——我有足够的钱过完余生。

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:19.910
<v David Diamond>I understand. But is it still in your best interest to make the most amount of money possible if you can find to do it as safely as possible.
<v David Diamond>我理解。但如果你能找到尽可能安全地做这件事的方法，尽可能多赚钱是否仍然符合你的最大利益？

00:30:19.910 --> 00:30:23.840
<v Marge>It's my obligation not to lose what I have.
<v Marge>我有义务不失去我所拥有的。

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:27.530
<v David Diamond>Correct. But it's also your obligation to keep your money working for you, otherwise, what's the point.
<v David Diamond>正确。但你也有义务让你的钱为你工作，否则，那样做的意义是什么。

00:30:27.530 --> 00:31:10.260
<v Nancy Updike>When I heard this part of the tape, even though I knew that this woman, this particular woman, was not getting conned. That she was, in fact, conning him and trapping him, I started to get so angry. Because I was thinking, he is really trying to take all of this old woman's money. All of it. She's saying, I have enough to live on. He's saying, you have an obligation to make more. Do you ever hear things like that and just get angry, even though you know that she's in on the con, on the joke?
<v Nancy Updike>当我听到录音的这一部分时，尽管我知道这个女人，这个特定的女人，并没有被骗。事实上，她在欺骗他，困住他，我开始变得非常愤怒。因为我在想，他真的在试图拿走这个老妇人的所有钱。全部。她说，我有足够的钱过日子。他说，你有义务赚更多。你有没有听到过这样的事情，即使你知道她在参与这个骗局，参与这个玩笑，仍然会感到愤怒？

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:44.260
<v Dale Sekovich>Every time I hear these pitches I'm outraged. Because I am the person that spoke to people who really did send David Diamond tens of thousands of dollars that consisted of their life savings and now don't have any money, even to buy groceries. I've interviewed them. I've seen them sob. Yeah, it makes me very angry.
<v Dale Sekovich>每次我听到这些推销话术，我都会感到愤怒。因为我是那个与真正给大卫·戴安德寄去数万美元的人交谈过的人，这些钱是他们的积蓄，现在他们连买杂货的钱都没有了。我采访过他们。我看着他们啜泣。是的，这让我非常愤怒。

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:47.190
<v Nancy Updike>And what sort of recourse do they have?
<v Nancy Updike>他们有什么追索权吗？

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:54.120
<v Dale  Sekovich>Slim and none. Slim and none.
<v Dale  Sekovich>微乎其微。微乎其微。

00:31:54.120 --> 00:32:22.170
<v David Diamond>We have public companies that want to take you public. So if you've got a public company that's passed judgement on it, it's not even me talking anymore. If Mark Erickson wants to do business with you it's not even me talking anymore. You have the ability to make an absolute fortune, and it doesn't make sense not to have every nickel you've got in this particular program. That's why I said it's an emergency investment situation and you should do at least fifty to a hundred, a hundred and fifty units while you have the opportunity.
<v David Diamond>我们有想让你上市的上市公司。所以如果你有一家上市公司对其进行了评判，那甚至不需要我再说话了。如果马克·埃里克森想和你做生意，那甚至不需要我再说话了。你有能力赚取一笔绝对的财富，不把你的每一分钱都投入这个特定的项目是不合理的。这就是为什么我说这是一个紧急的投资情况，你应该在有机会的时候至少做五十到一百，一百五十个单位。

00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:25.890
<v Marge>Well, how much are you investing in this?
<v Marge>嗯，你在这个项目中投资了多少？

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:38.620
<v David Diamond>I'm not investing anything in this. My investment comes in the time that I put with my clients. Right. And the fact that when they make money, they reinvest with me.
<v David Diamond>我没有在其中投资任何东西。我的投资体现在我为客户花费的时间上。没错。而且事实是，当他们赚钱时，他们会和我一起重新投资。

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:39.890
<v Marge>Right.
<v Marge>没错。

00:32:39.890 --> 00:32:41.680
<v David Diamond>That's the whole point.
<v David Diamond>这就是全部意义所在。

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:52.400
<v Nancy Updike>You were smiling again. When she said how much are you investing. Is she just screwing around with him?
<v Nancy Updike>你又在笑了。当她说你投资了多少时。她是在耍他吗？

00:32:52.400 --> 00:33:02.940
<v Dale Sekovich>Of course, she's playing with him. Yeah. I mean, she's trained to ask him those kinds of questions so that he responds with a misrepresentation.
<v Dale Sekovich>当然，她在逗他。我的意思是，她受过训练，问他这类问题，这样他就会做出虚假的陈述。

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:10.770
<v Nancy Updike>But that doesn't sound like it's part of the script. That just sounds like her being mean in sort of a delicious way.
<v Nancy Updike>但这听起来不像是剧本的一部分。这听起来只是她以一种美妙的方式在刻薄他。

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:30.640
<v Dale Sekovich>Well, no. I think what we were trying to do, or her handler was trying to do, was get her to get him to say, oh yeah, I'm in it, and I've got my mother and my grandmother in it. And I'm putting away money for my child's education with it. Because then we could show later that he hadn't.
<v Dale Sekovich>嗯，不。我想我们试图做的是，或者她的处理人员试图做的是，让她让他说，哦是的，我投了，我让我母亲和祖母也投了。我正用它为我孩子的教育存钱。因为那样我们稍后就能证明他没有。

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:45.420
<v Nancy Updike>Did you ever talk to Marge about what it's like to do this. Do they ever sort of have fun, just thinking, I'm just turning the tables on this guy. He has no idea.
<v Nancy Updike>你有没有和马吉谈过做这件事是什么感觉。他们有没有以此为乐，只是想，我只是在这个家伙身上反转了局面。他完全不知道。

00:33:45.420 --> 00:33:56.620
<v Dale Sekovich>I wish I could answer the question. I've never spoken to them. I'd love to get the answer to that myself. I'd like to ask that question myself. I think they get a lot of personal satisfaction though.
<v Dale Sekovich>我希望我能回答这个问题。我从未和他们交谈过。我也很想自己得到答案。我想亲自问这个问题。不过，我认为他们获得了很大的个人满足感。

00:33:56.620 --> 00:34:01.240
<v Nancy  Updike>How often do you get a chance to catch a bad guy as just a regular civilian.
<v Nancy  Updike>作为一个普通平民，你有多少机会抓到一个坏蛋。

00:34:01.240 --> 00:34:08.590
<v Dale Sekovich>Yeah, exactly. I do it too, I tape people using an alias in cases that I work.
<v Dale Sekovich>是的，没错。我也做，我在工作中处理案件时会使用化名录音。

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:10.080
<v Nancy Updike>And is it fun?
<v Nancy Updike>那很有趣吗？

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:19.700
<v Dale Sekovich>I love it. I love to get these people to tell me stuff. It's like acting. There's a rush.
<v Dale Sekovich>我喜欢它。我喜欢让这些人告诉我事情。就像演戏一样。有一种冲动感。

00:34:19.700 --> 00:35:13.140
<v Nancy Updike>The rush of a con, the pleasure of it, is knowing that you have more power than the person you're conning. You know more. You know that it's a con. And let's face it, given the choice between being the mark and being the con man, nobody's going to choose to be the mark. But the problem is, the more confidence you have in your own con, the more easily you become a mark yourself. Con men get taken by other con men all the time. There just seems to be something about the particular arrogance of always being on the knowing side of the con that makes for a really, really good mark.
<v Nancy Updike>骗局的冲动，它的乐趣，在于知道你比你正在欺骗的人拥有更多的权力。你知道更多。你知道这是一个骗局。说实话，在成为受害者和成为骗子之间做出选择时，没人会选择成为受害者。但问题是，你对自己的骗局越有信心，你就越容易成为受害者。骗子总是被其他骗子欺骗。总是站在骗局的知情一方，这种特定的傲慢似乎使得人们成为一个非常、非常好的受害者目标。

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:36.050
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike, in Los Angeles. Coming up, the thrill of sitting close to the stage, and the evil that that can lead to. In a minute, for Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>洛杉矶的南希·厄普代克。接下来，坐在舞台附近的快感，以及这可能导致的邪恶。一分钟后，为了国际公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:53.850
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, pulling a hoax on yourself. Stories of people who think that they are fooling others until they're not anymore.
<v Ira Glass>这是《这就是美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。当然，我们每周在节目中都会选择一个主题，并为您带来各种不同的相关故事。今天的节目，对自己进行欺骗。关于那些认为自己在愚弄别人，直到他们不再愚弄别人的故事。

00:35:53.850 --> 00:36:21.710
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at act three of our program. Act three, Outperforming the Performers. It's a measure of just how hungry people are to create a community that a full fledged, tight knit group of friends could form out of a publicity stunt. Back in 1996, a new musical called Rent was moving from a small theater in New York's East Village to the big time, a Broadway house just a half block from Times Square. The show presented a romanticized view of what it means to be young and broke.
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，《超越表演者》。人们建立社区的渴望程度是一个衡量标准，一个成熟、紧密的友谊群体竟然可以源于一个宣传噱头。回到1996年，一部名为《吉屋出租》的新音乐剧正在从纽约东村的一个小剧院搬到大舞台，一个距离时代广场仅半个街区的百老汇剧院。该剧呈现了一种浪漫化的视角，描绘了年轻且贫穷意味着什么。

00:36:21.710 --> 00:36:49.340
<v Ira Glass>And to keep the magical air of youth and poverty in the house, the producers decided that every single night they would sell two rows of seats, the first two rows, some of the best seats in the house, for a price nearly anyone could scrape together. $20 a pop, less than a third of what tickets normally sell for on Broadway. Hundreds of teens and twenty somethings took the bait. So many, that if you wanted to get one of the cheap seats, you had to arrive at six o'clock a night before, get in line with a sleeping bag, and stay out all night.
<v Ira Glass>为了保持剧院中那充满青春和贫穷的魔幻氛围，制片人决定每晚卖两排座位，前两排，剧院中最好的座位，价格几乎任何人都能凑出来。每张20美元，不到百老汇正常票价的三分之一。数百名十几岁和二十几岁的年轻人上钩了。人数之多，如果你想买到一张廉价票，你必须在前一晚六点到达，带着睡袋排队，在外面待上一整晚。

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:53.700
<v Joe  Gillis>See where they're standing? That's where we used to sleep.
<v Joe  Gillis>看到他们站的地方了吗？那就是我们以前睡觉的地方。

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:56.100
<v Rebecca Allen>We would sleep all where those photos are.
<v Rebecca Allen>我们就睡在那些照片所在的所有地方。

00:36:56.100 --> 00:37:05.110
<v Ira Glass>Joe Gillis and Rebecca Allen stand in front of the theater where Rent is still playing, near a row of life-sized pictures of the cast, mounted on a wall by the theater door.
<v Ira Glass>乔·吉利斯和丽贝卡·艾伦站在剧院前，那里还在演《吉屋出租》，靠近剧院门边墙上安装的一排真人大小的演员照片。

00:37:05.110 --> 00:37:09.680
<v Rebecca Allen>During the show we had to stay out there because they had to keep the marquee area clear.
<v Rebecca Allen>演出期间我们必须待在外面，因为他们必须保持天幕区域清洁。

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:17.470
<v Joe Gillis>So as soon as the show was over and they closed the doors, we'd all run over, and we all like, claim our spot because we'd all want to sleep under our favorite cast member.
<v Joe Gillis>所以演出一结束，他们关上门，我们就会跑过去，我们都会像这样，认领我们的位置，因为我们都想睡在我们最喜欢的演员下方。

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:19.480
<v Ira Glass>And so which one was yours?
<v Ira Glass>那么你是哪一个？

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:27.220
<v Joe Gillis>I slept under Daphne once and I think I slept under Anthony another time.
<v Joe Gillis>我有一次睡在达芙妮下面，我想另一次我睡在安东尼下面。

00:37:27.220 --> 00:37:37.730
<v Rebecca  Allen>You're sleeping on the pavement while people walk by, ignoring you. And you're like, yeah, I'm just like those people in the play. You don't really think that.
<v Rebecca  Allen>你睡在人行道上，路人走过，无视你。然后你就像，是的，我就像剧中的那些人一样。你并不是真的那么想。

00:37:37.730 --> 00:37:39.490
<v Ira Glass>But a small part of you thinks that.
<v Ira Glass>但你内心的一小部分会这么想。

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:47.770
<v Rebecca Allen>A small part of you thinks that. And will identify more with the play, the more you can be like it.
<v Rebecca Allen>你内心的一小部分会这么想。而且你会越像它，就越认同这部剧。

00:37:47.770 --> 00:38:32.870
<v Ira   Glass>When they were in line, everybody would hang out all day. Grab some food at McDonald's or at the diner across the street. And the fans who didn't show up at the theater on any given day would communicate through an email list with hundreds of Rent heads on it. Most of them were suburban kids like Joe and Rebecca. Students. Joe says that he was closer to the Rent fans than to his friends back in high school. It was on the Rent line that he first came out as gay, long before his friends back home knew. For lots of teenagers, this was the first community that felt like their own.
<v Ira   Glass>排队时，大家整天都会待在一起。在麦当劳或街对面的餐馆吃点东西。那些没有去剧院的粉丝会通过一个有数百名《吉屋出租》粉丝的电子邮件列表进行交流。他们大多数是像乔和丽贝卡那样的郊区孩子。学生。乔说他与《吉屋出租》粉丝的关系比他在高中的朋友更亲密。就是在排队买票时，他第一次公开了自己是同性恋，远在老家的朋友知道之前。对于许多青少年来说，这是第一个让他们感到归属的社区。

00:38:32.870 --> 00:39:13.200
<v Ira   Glass>It's easy to see why they like the show which unironically celebrates what it unironically calls the bohemian life, in French. A life of poverty and love and art. The setting is contemporary New York. The characters are homosexuals, homeless people, interracial couples, drug addicts, flailing artists, and doomed HIV positive lovers. The mood of the show is at once fantastically idealistic and desperately tragic. Several characters are dying of AIDS. And among the throngs of young people who are drawn to the magnetic pool of Rent's message that you should live each day like it's your only day on Earth was a young student who we'll call Stephanie. All of our interviewees also agreed to call her Stephanie.
<v Ira   Glass>很容易理解他们为什么喜欢这部剧，它毫不讽刺地庆祝着它毫不讽刺地称为“波西米亚生活”的生活方式。一种贫穷、爱与艺术的生活。背景是现代纽约。角色是同性恋、无家可归者、跨种族情侣、吸毒者、挣扎的艺术家，以及注定感染艾滋病的恋人。这部剧的情绪既极其理想主义，又极其悲惨。几个角色死于艾滋病。在那些被《吉屋出租》关于“你应该把每一天都当作你在地球上的最后一天”的信息所吸引的年轻人群中，有一位年轻的学生，我们称之为斯蒂芬妮。我们所有的受访者也都同意称她为斯蒂芬妮。

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:27.660
<v Catherine Skidmore>We met about two and a half, three years ago, when we were both interested in the musical Rent. We belonged to the same mailing list online. And she drove up to New York with some friends from college and they stayed in my apartment while they went to see the show.
<v Catherine Skidmore>我们大概两年前，两年前半相识，那时我们都对音乐剧《吉屋出租》感兴趣。我们属于同一个在线邮件列表。她和几个大学的朋友开车去纽约，在看戏期间住在我公寓里。

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:48.060
<v Ira Glass>This is Catherine Skidmore, a friend of Stephanie's and one of the ringleaders among the Rent fans. She was a founder of one of the big internet mailing lists about the show. She's young, used to run in computer hacker circles. One of her tattoos is her social security number in binary code. Now she works for a web start up. Stephanie was one of the many people she got to know, back during the heyday of Rent.
<v Ira Glass>这是凯瑟琳·斯基德莫尔，斯蒂芬妮的朋友，也是《吉屋出租》粉丝中的核心人物之一。她是关于该剧的大型互联网邮件列表的创始人之一。她很年轻，以前在电脑黑客圈子里混过。她的纹身之一是她用二进制代码写的社会安全号码。现在她为一家网络创业公司工作。斯蒂芬妮是她在《吉屋出租》全盛时期结识的众多人之一。

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:56.410
<v Catherine Skidmore>I lived the closest to the theater out of anybody. And I think I was the only person in the line who had her own apartment, so I used to let friends stay with me when they drove up.
<v Catherine Skidmore>我住得离剧院最近。我想我是排队的人里唯一有自己公寓的，所以我经常让朋友开车上来时住在我那儿。

00:39:56.410 --> 00:39:58.000
<v Ira Glass>How often would you let somebody stay with you?
<v Ira Glass>你多久会让别人住你那儿一次？

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:03.090
<v Catherine Skidmore>I think every weekend or every other weekend. It got turned into the hotel Catherine.
<v Catherine Skidmore>我想每个周末或隔周一次。它变成了“凯瑟琳酒店”。

00:40:03.090 --> 00:40:13.670
<v Ira Glass>She says Stephanie let everyone know that she had a medical condition called autonomic neuropathy. And she said it was terminal. She told this people on the line and she told it to members of the cast of Rent.
<v Ira Glass>她说斯蒂芬妮让所有人都知道她患有一种叫自主神经病变的疾病。她说那是绝症。她告诉了排队的人，也告诉了《吉屋出租》的演员们。

00:40:13.670 --> 00:40:31.880
<v Catherine Skidmore>When she was in New York she used to come to the stage door and ask the actors to give her tours backstage, or ask them out for lunch or dinner. When she would email us Stephanie would say, I'm not feeling great. Could you ask the cast to send me a card, could you ask them to email me.
<v Catherine Skidmore>她在纽约时，经常去后台入口要求演员带她参观后台，或者约他们出去吃午饭或晚饭。当她给我们发电子邮件时，斯蒂芬妮会说，“我感觉不太好。你能请演员们给我发张卡片吗，你能请他们给我发邮件吗？”

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:38.140
<v Anthony Rapp>I first got a letter that she kind of sent the same letter to several of us in the cast in New York.
<v Anthony Rapp>我最初收到了一封信，她基本上把同样的信发给了我们在纽约的几位演员。

00:40:38.140 --> 00:40:40.670
<v Ira Glass>Anthony Rapp played the lead role in Rent.
<v Ira Glass>安东尼·拉普在《吉屋出租》中担任主角。

00:40:40.670 --> 00:40:57.740
<v Anthony Rapp>She was a young aspiring actress, student who was suffering from an illness, and that the show brought her great comfort. And she didn't know how long she had to live. You know, my heart went out to her, as all of our hearts did.
<v Anthony Rapp>她是一位有抱负的年轻女演员，一个患有疾病的学生，这部剧带给了她极大的安慰。她不知道自己还能活多久。你知道，我的心向她敞开了，就像我们所有人的心一样。

00:40:57.740 --> 00:41:01.740
<v Gwen  Stewart>You know, my heart just went out to her. It really did.
<v Gwen  Stewart>你知道，我的心只是向她敞开了。真的。

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:04.160
<v Ira Glass>Gwen Stewart was another cast member.
<v Ira Glass>格温·斯图尔特是另一位演员。

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:27.960
<v Gwen Stewart>And whenever she came to the show I would make sure that I would try to spend a little time with her. We'd gone out to dinner a couple times, and we'd sit around and talk, and I called her on the phone. I gave her my home number. And it was to the point where I literally made a point of praying for this girl each and every night.
<v Gwen Stewart>每当她来看戏时，我都会确保试着花点时间陪她。我们出去吃过几次饭，我们就坐下来聊天，我给她打过电话。我把家里的号码给了她。后来甚至到了我真的特意每晚为这个女孩祈祷的地步。

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:40.820
<v Anthony Rapp>Well ultimately what we were hearing was that she was about to have this major surgery and that she was very scared about it. And that she came to New York right before she was going to have it. That was when Gwen Stewart and I went to lunch with her.
<v Anthony Rapp>嗯，最终我们听到的是她即将进行一次大手术，她对此感到非常害怕。她在做手术之前来到了纽约。那时格温·斯图尔特和我去和她吃了午餐。

00:41:40.820 --> 00:42:02.820
<v Gwen Stewart>She said that she was contemplating suicide. And I'm a spiritual person, and I'd gone as far as to look up all these scriptures to support life and to support hope and faith. And I would give them to her and tell her to read her Bible. And a lot of the conversations were like that. And then, you know, if it got a little too heavy, we'd swing it around to Rent.
<v Gwen Stewart>她说她有自杀的念头。我是一个有信仰的人，我甚至专门去查了所有支持生命、支持希望和信仰的经文。我会把它们给她，告诉她去读圣经。很多对话都是那样的。然后，你知道，如果气氛太沉重，我们就转到《吉屋出租》上去。

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:18.620
<v Anthony Rapp>And at that lunch she brought out some medical paraphernalia. Like a syringe, and like an IV tube thing. She went off to the bathroom to supposedly administer herself some medication.
<v Anthony Rapp>在午餐会上，她拿出了一些医疗器械。比如注射器，还有静脉输液管之类的东西。她去洗手间假装给自己用药。

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:30.880
<v Ira  Glass>After her lunch with the stars in New York, Stephanie flew back to the city where she was from, where she dropped in on a touring company of Rent which happened to be performing there.
<v Ira  Glass>在与纽约的明星们共进午餐后，斯蒂芬妮飞回了她的家乡城市，在那里她意外造访了恰好在那里演出的《吉屋出租》巡回剧团。

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:40.010
<v Anthony  Rapp>She was getting to know them and befriending them and telling them her story and they held a prayer circle for her the night before her supposed surgery.
<v Anthony  Rapp>她开始结识他们并与他们交朋友，告诉他们她的故事，他们在她所谓的“手术”前一晚为她举行了祈祷会。

00:42:40.010 --> 00:42:43.610
<v Ira Glass>You mean just backstage at the theater?
<v Ira Glass>你是说就在剧院的后台？

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:48.630
<v Anthony Rapp>Yeah, she was backstage and she was there with the cast and they held a prayer circle for her.
<v Anthony Rapp>是的，她在后台，和演员们在一起，他们为她举行了祈祷会。

00:42:48.630 --> 00:43:13.990
<v Ira  Glass>Perhaps you've already figured out where all this is going. In the end, Stephanie did not turn out of any kind of terminal condition. What's remarkable about her story is how far she was able to push the lie, and how compulsively she did it, almost like it was out of her control to stop herself. As she got sicker, her friends in New York started to get emails from someone named Monica, who claimed to be a friend of Stephanie's.
<v Ira  Glass>也许你已经猜到这一切要去向何方了。最终，斯蒂芬妮并没有患上任何绝症。她故事中非凡之处在于她能把谎言推到什么地步，以及她有多么强迫性地这样做，几乎到了她无法控制自己停止的地步。随着她“病情”加重，她在纽约的朋友开始收到一个叫莫妮卡的人发来的电子邮件，声称是斯蒂芬妮的朋友。

00:43:13.990 --> 00:43:37.740
<v Ira  Glass>It was Monica who wrote them when Stephanie went into surgery. Monica gave lengthy accounts of Stephanie's condition, and urged them to organize get well cards and calls from the cast. Catherine later collected all of Monica's and Stephanie's emails into a massive document that she calls the log of deception. With her roommate Jen Eldridge, who also met Stephanie on the Rent line and considered her a close friend, Catherine stood at her computer and scrolled through the log.
<v Ira  Glass>当斯蒂芬妮进入手术室时，正是莫妮卡写信给他们。莫妮卡提供了关于斯蒂芬妮病情的详细说明，并敦促他们组织演员们送上慰问卡片和电话。凯瑟琳后来把莫妮卡和斯蒂芬妮的所有邮件收集成了一份庞大的文件，她称之为“欺骗日志”。和她的室友简·埃尔德里奇——简也在排队时遇到了斯蒂芬妮，并认为她是好朋友——凯瑟琳站在电脑前浏览这本日志。

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:41.280
<v Jen Eldridge>Yeah, oh, where's my favorite one. Yeah, Monica emails are good.
<v Jen Eldridge>是的，哦，我最喜欢的那条在哪。是的，莫妮卡的邮件很棒。

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:43.950
<v Catherine Skidmore>Find the one about the coma. That's [INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Catherine Skidmore>找找关于昏迷的那条。那是 [交叉声音]

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:44.860
<v Jen Eldridge>The suicide one.
<v Jen Eldridge>自杀的那条。

00:43:44.860 --> 00:43:48.390
<v Catherine Skidmore>The suicide one's good, too. There's coma and suicide.
<v Catherine Skidmore>自杀的那条也很棒。有昏迷也有自杀。

00:43:48.390 --> 00:43:52.200
<v Ira Glass>They decide to go for suicide.
<v Ira Glass>她们决定看自杀的那条。

00:43:52.200 --> 00:44:19.290
<v Catherine Skidmore>Here we go. This is an email from Monica on Friday, November 7, 1997, at 9:00 PM. Where she says, she sent this directly to me. I'm at Stephanie's, I just followed the EMTs out to the ambulance. Stephanie tried to end it all tonight. I found a syringe and an empty container of morphine. She didn't know that I was coming to check on her, blah, blah, blah, they say she'll be OK, and I caught her just in time. She really went all out, and I don't know how much of this she planned or how much--
<v Catherine Skidmore>就是这儿。这是一封莫妮卡在1997年11月7日星期五晚上9点发来的邮件。她说，她直接发给我的。“我在斯蒂芬妮这儿，我刚跟着急救人员去了救护车。斯蒂芬妮今晚试图结束一切。我发现了一支注射器和一个空的吗啡容器。她不知道我会过来检查她，巴拉巴拉，他们说她会没事的，我及时发现并救了她。”她真的做了全套，我不知道她计划了多少，或者有多少——

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:37.190
<v Jen Eldridge>And keep in mind, this is a weekend she knew that all of her friends were converging in New York at Catherine's apartment. Like we were all going to be there together when this email came. And I think we were all in different levels of transit when you got this email and started just being very upset. And trying to get contact, and everybody's in cars and on trains trying to get there.
<v Jen Eldridge>记住，这个周末她知道她所有的朋友都汇聚在纽约凯瑟琳的公寓里。就像我们大家都要在一起时，收到了这封邮件。我想我们收到这封邮件并开始感到非常不安时，我们都在不同的交通工具上。大家都在车里和火车上试图赶过去。

00:44:37.190 --> 00:44:40.370
<v Ira Glass>And then what happen once you all knew that she was--
<v Ira Glass>那么当你们都知道她——之后发生了什么？

00:44:40.370 --> 00:44:47.700
<v Catherine Skidmore>We tried to call the hospitals, and we tried to call 911 to see if they had a record of it. I called Kenny.
<v Catherine Skidmore>我们试着打给医院，我们试着打911看看他们是否有记录。我打给肯尼。

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:50.220
<v Ira Glass>Oh, you tried to call her in a hospital and you couldn't find her.
<v Ira Glass>哦，你试着在医院找她，却找不到。

00:44:50.220 --> 00:45:00.800
<v Catherine Skidmore>We could find her. No hospitals in her area had records of her being checked in. So we just said, oh my god, she's somewhere and we can't get to her, and we don't know what to do. And nobody knew what to do.
<v Catherine Skidmore>找不到。她所在地区的医院都没有她入院的记录。所以我们就说，天哪，她在某处，我们联系不到她，不知道该怎么办。没人知道该怎么办。

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:06.220
<v Ira Glass>It's amazing how un-thought out it is. What did she think you were going to do once you got that email?
<v Ira Glass>这简直太没经过深思熟虑了。她以为你们收到那封邮件后会做什么？

00:45:06.220 --> 00:45:07.670
<v Catherine Skidmore>I don't know.
<v Catherine Skidmore>我不知道。

00:45:07.670 --> 00:45:13.520
<v Ira Glass>And so your evening together, like where all of you were going to be together hanging out, ended up being completely--
<v Ira Glass>所以你们在一起度过的那个晚上，就像你们大家本该聚在一起玩的时候，最后完全——

00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:25.560
<v Catherine Skidmore>It was pretty miserable. Yeah, it was really miserable. We went, I guess we saw the show. Everybody bawled their way through it and cried their way through it. And went and told the cast members, of course, because that's what we thought she'd want us to do.
<v Catherine Skidmore>那真是很惨。是的，真的很惨。我想我们去看了演出。每个人都在看戏过程中泪流满面，哭着看完的。当然，我们去告诉了演员们，因为我们以为那是她希望我们做的。

00:45:25.560 --> 00:45:50.390
<v Ira Glass>There was one person who always got Monica's story. Josh Safran, who at the time was boyfriends with the star of Rent, Anthony Rapp. Josh not only doubted that Stephanie was sick, he felt horribly guilty for doubting her. This was his problem in a nutshell, he says. Anthony was all about trusting and loving people. He was all about cynicism and mistrust. Fortunately for him, his friends Sean and Michelle also doubted Stephanie.
<v Ira Glass>有一个人总是能看穿莫妮卡的故事。乔什·萨夫兰，当时是《吉屋出租》明星安东尼·拉普的男朋友。乔什不仅怀疑斯蒂芬妮生病，他还因为怀疑她而感到极度内疚。他说，这就是他问题的症结所在。安东尼满心都是信任和爱别人。他满心都是愤世嫉俗和不信任。幸运的是，他的朋友肖恩和米歇尔也怀疑斯蒂芬妮。

00:45:50.390 --> 00:45:58.000
<v Josh Safran>So we were suspicious. We were talking more about it for a few days, but then she goes to the hospital, and she goes into a coma.
<v Josh Safran>所以我们很怀疑。我们讨论了几天，但随后她进了医院，陷入了昏迷。

00:45:58.000 --> 00:46:06.550
<v Ira Glass>That is, an email from Monica said Stephanie was in a coma. And when this happened Catherine, who wasn't doubting at all, made a decision.
<v Ira Glass>也就是说，莫妮卡的一封邮件说斯蒂芬妮昏迷了。当这件事发生时，完全不怀疑的凯瑟琳做了一个决定。

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:25.420
<v Josh Safran>I talked to Catherine, and Catherine was like, even though she doesn't want us to go there, even though Stephanie doesn't want any visitors, we're going to go. She's in a coma. We should be there. And I said good. And I said, tell Monica. And as soon as they told Monica, Stephanie woke up. As soon as that happened, Sean and Michelle and I were positive that we got her.
<v Josh Safran>我和凯瑟琳谈过，凯瑟琳说，尽管她不想让我们去，尽管斯蒂芬妮不想有任何访客，我们还是要去看。她昏迷了。我们应该在那儿。我说好。我说，告诉莫妮卡。就在他们告诉莫妮卡之后，斯蒂芬妮醒了。这件事一发生，肖恩、米歇尔和我肯定我们识破她了。

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:28.640
<v Ira Glass>Why? Why, what did that mean to you that she woke up at that moment.
<v Ira Glass>为什么？为什么，她在那一刻醒来对你们意味着什么？

00:46:28.640 --> 00:46:40.860
<v Josh Safran>She woke up so they didn't have to come. That was Monica, the way she phrased it in the email was, oh, she's better, you don't have to come. She'd like to see you in a few days. It was so obvious that she was pushing off. So we needed to find a way to make sure.
<v Josh Safran>她醒了，所以他们就不用去了。那是莫妮卡在邮件里的措辞，“哦，她好了，你们不用来了。她过几天想见你们。”很明显，她在推托。所以我们需要找一种方法来确认。

00:46:40.860 --> 00:46:56.650
<v Ira Glass>How to make sure. Well, it turns out that we live in a country where, if there's an emergency requiring a plumber or an electrician or a mechanic, people turn to professionals. But when it comes to detective work, deep down, we all believe we're capable of it.
<v Ira Glass>如何确认。嗯，事实证明，我们生活在一个国家，如果出现需要水管工、电工或机械师的紧急情况，人们会求助于专业人士。但当涉及到侦探工作时，在内心深处，我们都认为自己是有能力的。

00:46:56.650 --> 00:47:10.110
<v Josh Safran>It really became a crusade. I mean, there were three days where none of us slept. We only worked on this. And that was insane. It's embarrassing to think about it.
<v Josh Safran>这真的成了一场十字军东征。我的意思是，有三天我们都没睡觉。我们只做了这件事。那太疯狂了。想到这事真让人尴尬。

00:47:10.110 --> 00:47:31.270
<v Ira Glass>They called dozens of hospitals in the city where Stephanie lived, asking if she was a patient. They researched the disease she supposedly had, they pored over old emails looking for clues. Finally they decided to call the elusive Monica who no one had ever spoken to. It turned out that there was in fact a real Monica who went to school with Stephanie, and after some spade work, they found her real last name.
<v Ira Glass>他们打了几十家斯蒂芬妮所在城市的医院，问她是否是病人。他们研究了她所谓的疾病，他们仔细阅读了旧邮件寻找线索。最后他们决定给那个没人跟她说过话、难以捉摸的莫妮卡打电话。事实证明，确实有一个和斯蒂芬妮一起上学的真实莫妮卡，在做了一些调查工作后，他们找到了她的真实姓氏。

00:47:31.270 --> 00:48:00.650
<v Josh Safran>We called information, and there was that number. She existed, she was there, we immediately were like, oh [BLEEP]. This was the moment of crisis where we were running around the apartment, freaking out, thinking we are terrible people. Because if we call this number, and this girl's like yes, Stephanie's in the hospital, but she's much better, then we were terrible, cynical people for-- like, it was everything that is wrong with the world and wrong with us. How could we ever think this. How could we spend so much time on it, how could we be such a horrible person.
<v Josh Safran>我们查了电话号码，确实有那个号码。她确实存在，她就在那儿，我们立刻就想，哦[脏话]。这是危机的时刻，我们在公寓里跑来跑去，吓坏了，认为我们是可怕的人。因为如果我们打了这个号码，这个女孩说“是的，斯蒂芬妮在医院，但她好多了”，那么我们就是可怕的、愤世嫉俗的人——就像，这是世界上所有错误和我们身上所有错误的东西。我们怎么会那样想。我们怎么能花那么多时间在这上面，我们怎么能成为这么可怕的人。

00:48:00.650 --> 00:48:44.720
<v Josh Safran>So I called the number, and Monica answered. And I was so nervous. I remember it was in my dingy little kitchen with the plastic countertop, and I was so nervous that I was shaking the plastic countertop, and things were falling all over. And Monica was like, I've heard about you, but there's all this loud noise. So nervous. I said, how's Stephanie? And she said, she's doing better. And I was like, OK, thank you. And I hung up the phone because I didn't want-- I couldn't deal with it. But immediately Michelle, Sean, and I were like, she's doing better, she really is sick. But then we thought, but wait a minute, maybe not.
<v Josh Safran>所以我打了那个号码，莫妮卡接了。我非常紧张。我记得是在我肮脏的小厨房里，台面是塑料的，我紧张得在摇晃那个塑料台面，东西掉了一地。莫妮卡说，“我听说过你，但这里有很大的噪音。”我太紧张了。我说，“斯蒂芬妮怎么样了？”她说，“她好多了。”我说，“好的，谢谢。”然后我挂了电话，因为我不想——我应付不了。但米歇尔、肖恩和我立刻觉得，“她说她好多了，她真的病了。”但后来我们想，“等等，也许没病。”

00:48:44.720 --> 00:49:03.380
<v Josh Safran>So they were like, you have to call back. And I'm like, I'm not going to call back. We just found out we're horrible people, she really is ill, blah, blah, blah. So they were like, but she didn't sound concerned, she didn't sound worried, call back. So we waited 10 minutes then I called back and I was like hi, I'm sorry Monica, it's Josh again. Look, when's the last time you saw Stephanie? She said, I saw her in class this morning. And that was it.
<v Josh Safran>所以他们说，“你必须打回去。”我说，“我不打了。我们刚发现我们是可怕的人，她真的病了，巴拉巴拉。”所以他们说，“但她听起来并不担心，她听起来不忧虑，打回去。”所以我们等了10分钟，然后我打了回去，我说：“嗨，抱歉莫妮卡，我是乔什。听着，你上次见斯蒂芬妮是什么时候？”她说：“我今天早上在课上见到她了。”事情就是这样。

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:19.010
<v Ira Glass>They contacted Stephanie, let her know that they had talked to the real Monica. They knew there was no coma. After some hedging, Stephanie finally admitted she wasn't dying. The next week, Catherine used her plane ticket and went to talk to Stephanie in person.
<v Ira Glass>他们联系了斯蒂芬妮，让她知道他们已经和真正的莫妮卡谈过了。他们知道并没有什么昏迷。经过一些闪烁其词，斯蒂芬妮终于承认她并没有快要死了。接下来的星期，凯瑟琳用了她的机票去亲自找斯蒂芬妮谈话。

00:49:19.010 --> 00:49:42.660
<v Catherine Skidmore>I had a list compiled of questions from our friends on why she did this, how sick was she, and she basically admitted that she did this as a cry for attention of sorts and it went overboard. And then once she started telling us all of these lies about what was going on she didn't have any way to back out of it. Then she kept going and she didn't feel like she could stop at any point during this.
<v Catherine Skidmore>我整理了一份朋友们的问题清单，问她为什么要这样做，她病得有多重，她基本上承认了她是出于一种寻求关注的方式才这样做的，而且做得过头了。然后一旦她开始编造所有关于发生什么事的谎言，她就无法回头了。然后她继续这样做，在这过程中她觉得自己在任何时候都停不下来。

00:49:42.660 --> 00:49:53.900
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie declined a request to be interviewed for this radio story. Jen remembers that before everybody knew the truth about Stephanie, it was a regular thing to get panicky calls from her between midnight and 2:00 AM.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮拒绝了接受这个广播故事采访的请求。简记得，在每个人都知道斯蒂芬妮真相之前，半夜两点到凌晨两点接到她的恐慌电话是家常便饭。

00:49:53.900 --> 00:50:42.120
<v Jen Eldridge>She would call me crying in the middle of the night that she was just ready to kill herself, couldn't deal with stuff. And I honestly think that when she called me and said these things, that it was genuine. It was just sort of something that went along with it and we would talk about it when she needed to and needed to be upset and cry. And there were many conversations about how she just didn't know how to deal with it anymore. And as I look back on it and I wonder if some of those I don't know how to deal with it conversations were really about she didn't know how to tell people that she had done this. I think there was a point for her when she realized that this was going to blow up in her face.
<v Jen Eldridge>她会在半夜哭着打电话给我说她准备自杀，处理不了那些事情。我真的认为当她打电话给我说这些时，那是真诚的。这只是伴随它而来的一部分，我们需要时就会谈论它，需要心烦和哭泣时就会谈论。有过很多对话关于她只是不知道如何处理它了。回想起来，我想知道那些“我不知道如何处理它”的对话是否真的是关于她不知道如何告诉人们她做了这些。我觉得对她来说有一个时刻，她意识到这会让她自己陷入麻烦。

00:50:42.120 --> 00:51:00.240
<v Ira          Glass>A word now about the plot of the musical Rent. It is, of course, about terminal illness. One character dies, another nearly dies. Anthony Rapp.
<v Ira          Glass>现在说一下音乐剧《吉屋出租》的剧情。当然，它是关于绝症的。一个角色死了，另一个差点死了。安东尼·拉普。

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:23.510
<v Anthony Rapp>It's very much about what people do in the face of all that. And in the case of the show, the group of friends really bands together. And so I don't know. I guess maybe on some level Stephanie thought that we would appreciate the plight of somebody who was young and facing something dire like that.
<v Anthony Rapp>它非常关于人们在面对所有这些时会做什么。在这部剧的例子中，朋友们真的团结在了一起。所以我不确定。我想也许在某种程度上，斯蒂芬妮认为我们会同情一个年轻人面临那种严重情况的困境。

00:51:23.510 --> 00:51:25.590
<v Ira Glass>And then everybody would band around.
<v Ira Glass>然后每个人都会团结起来。

00:51:25.590 --> 00:51:26.240
<v Anthony Rapp>Yeah.
<v Anthony Rapp>是的。

00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:31.990
<v Ira Glass>In a way it's like she's casting herself in the show.
<v Ira Glass>在某种程度上，就像她把自己投射到了剧里一样。

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:34.300
<v Anthony Rapp>Yeah, sure.
<v Anthony Rapp>是的，当然。

00:51:34.300 --> 00:51:42.920
<v Ira Glass>In a way, it's like, what does a fan want from a show. Especially the kind of fan who comes back again and again. It's like somehow they want to get closer to it. They want to get inside it. They want to be in it.
<v Ira Glass>在某种程度上，就像，粉丝想从剧中得到什么。尤其是那种一遍又一遍回来的粉丝。就像他们想以某种方式更接近它。他们想进入它。他们想身处其中。

00:51:42.920 --> 00:51:51.210
<v Anthony Rapp>I know when I see a show that I love, I want to be in it. Seriously. It brought out the best in people. It did.
<v Anthony Rapp>我知道当我看到一部我喜爱的剧时，我想身处其中。说真的。它激发了人们最好的一面。确实如此。

00:51:51.210 --> 00:52:22.870
<v Ira  Glass>In fact, the script of the show provided a model for everyone on how to act with Stephanie. Both for the fans who saw the show dozens of times and the cast who sung out their hearts every night about the importance of sticking by your friends when they're dying. Stephanie got her wish. Not only did she get sympathy and attention, she got it from the very people who she watched on stage, in Rent, time and again. When Catherine went back through her old emails, she noticed how often Stephanie was asking to deliver a message to the cast, or asking about the cast.
<v Ira  Glass>事实上，该剧的剧本为每个人提供了如何对待斯蒂芬妮的榜样。无论是对看了几十遍的粉丝，还是对每晚在舞台上唱出他们对朋友临终时陪伴的重要性的演员来说。斯蒂芬妮如愿以偿了。她不仅得到了同情和关注，还得到了她一遍又一遍在《吉屋出租》中观看的那些人给予的。当凯瑟琳回顾她的旧邮件时，她注意到斯蒂芬妮是多么频繁地要求向演员传递信息，或者询问关于演员的情况。

00:52:22.870 --> 00:52:34.890
<v Catherine Skidmore>Afterwards, it was just pretty sick to read back and see it. That she really didn't care about her-- we thought we were her friends. She really cared more about the cast and their reaction.
<v Catherine Skidmore>后来，回读这些真的很令人作呕。她根本不在乎她的——我们以为我们是她的朋友。她真的更在乎演员和他们的反应。

00:52:34.890 --> 00:52:40.430
<v Ira Glass>You know the point in the show where Angel dies and then all the other characters come forward and give little tributes.
<v Ira Glass>你知道剧中天使死的时候，然后所有其他角色上前进行一点致敬的那一段吗。

00:52:40.430 --> 00:52:41.890
<v Catherine Skidmore>Yeah, a eulogy.
<v Catherine Skidmore>是的，葬礼演讲。

00:52:41.890 --> 00:52:46.620
<v Ira Glass>Give little eulogies. Do you think that Stephanie fantasized about all of you-- you do.
<v Ira Glass>进行小小的悼词。你认为斯蒂芬妮幻想过你们所有人——你是这么想的。

00:52:46.620 --> 00:52:59.530
<v Catherine Skidmore>Oh, absolutely. She had emailed us and said, when I go, when they put me in the ground, I want so and so to sing the reprise of "I'll Cover You" from the show at my grave. It would be great.
<v Catherine Skidmore>哦，绝对。她给我们发过邮件说，“当我走了，当他们把我埋在土里时，我想要某某人在这部剧的重唱部分在我的坟墓前演唱‘我会覆盖你’”。那会很棒。

00:52:59.530 --> 00:53:17.920
<v Gwen                          Stewart>When I first realized that it was all a lie, I was very angry.
<v Gwen                          Stewart>当我第一次意识到这一切都是谎言时，我非常生气。

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:20.590
<v Ira  Glass>Again, cast member Gwen Stewart.
<v Ira  Glass>演员格温·斯图尔特再次说道。

00:53:20.590 --> 00:53:30.570
<v Gwen Stewart>Very angry. And that's why I didn't speak to any of them. Because I had allowed a bunch of strangers to come into my life and I'd opened my heart to them.
<v Gwen Stewart>非常生气。这就是为什么我没有和他们任何人说话。因为我让一群陌生人进入了我的生活，我向他们敞开了心扉。

00:53:30.570 --> 00:53:35.240
<v Ira  Glass>After this, did you change your attitude toward the fans in general?
<v Ira  Glass>在那之后，你对粉丝的态度总体上改变了吗？

00:53:35.240 --> 00:54:06.030
<v Gwen Stewart>Not really. I mean, I didn't give out my number anymore. But I still signed autographs. I still took the time to talk to people, and took pictures, and all that other stuff. I didn't open myself anymore like I did. I felt betrayed. And I had to make sure that I didn't allow anybody else to do that.
<v Gwen Stewart>没有。我的意思是，我不再给号码了。但我仍然签售。我仍然花时间与人交谈，拍照，做所有那些事。我不再像那样敞开心扉了。我感到背叛。我必须确保我没有让其他人那样做。

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:11.250
<v Ira Glass>After all this happened, did you want to pray for her, still.
<v Ira Glass>在这之后，你还想为她祈祷吗。

00:54:11.250 --> 00:54:21.980
<v Gwen  Stewart>Oh, I did. I did. Because I realized that sickness and death were the least of her problems.
<v Gwen  Stewart>哦，我有。我有。因为我意识到疾病和死亡是她最轻的问题。

00:54:21.980 --> 00:54:33.390
<v Ira Glass>Back on 41st Street, Rebecca and Joe, who knew Stephanie, but not all that well, say that Stephanie was just doing a more extreme version of what a lot of people did on the line.
<v Ira Glass>回到第41街，认识斯蒂芬妮但并不太熟的丽贝卡和乔说，斯蒂芬妮只是在做很多人排队时所做事情的更极端版本。

00:54:33.390 --> 00:54:51.380
<v Rebecca  Allen>It's not surprising to find out that something you think about someone in the line is a lie, or just like, a little off. Sasha, please, don't get me started. For example, this is my good friend Joe Gillis. But that is not his name. And how long did I know you before I knew that was not your name.
<v Rebecca  Allen>发现你在排队时认为关于某人的事情是谎言，或者只是稍微有点出入，这并不令人惊讶。萨沙，拜托，别跟我提这个。例如，这是我的好朋友乔·吉利斯。但那不是他的名字。我认识你多久才知道那不是你的名字。

00:54:51.380 --> 00:54:55.240
<v Joe Gillis>When did you figure out that was not my name?
<v Joe Gillis>你什么时候发现那不是我的名字的？

00:54:55.240 --> 00:54:56.620
<v Ira Glass>What is your name?
<v Ira Glass>你叫什么名字？

00:54:56.620 --> 00:55:01.000
<v Joe  Gillis>My name is Joe Falduti.
<v Joe  Gillis>我的名字是乔·法尔杜蒂。

00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:11.510
<v Ira Glass>On the line there was a Bridget, whose real name was Laura, a 15-year-old who everybody thought was 22, and many gay teenagers, who are out only on the neutral ground of 41st Street.
<v Ira Glass>在队伍里有个布里奇特，真名是劳拉，一个大家都以为是22岁的15岁女孩，还有许多同性恋青少年，他们只在第41街的中立地带出柜。

00:55:11.510 --> 00:55:25.880
<v Joe Gillis>I mean, I actively knew it was not real life. And I actually would say, my Rent friends and my real friends. But it's really weird how something that was so not real affected real life so much.
<v Joe Gillis>我的意思是，我积极地知道这不是真实生活。我甚至会说，“我的《吉屋出租》朋友和我的现实朋友”。但真的很奇怪，某种完全不真实的东西竟然对真实生活产生了这么大的影响。

00:55:25.880 --> 00:55:28.420
<v Ira  Glass>In a way, a play does too.
<v Ira  Glass>在某种程度上，戏剧也是如此。

00:55:28.420 --> 00:55:40.290
<v Rebecca  Allen>That's what theater wants to do. They want you to become emotionally involved and care about something, and then leave it and go back to your real life.
<v Rebecca  Allen>那正是戏剧想要做的。他们想让你在情感上投入并关心某件事，然后离开它，回到你的现实生活中。

00:55:40.290 --> 00:56:20.810
<v Ira Glass>Which is what Stephanie did on the Rent line. She got everyone emotionally involved, made them care about something. And then, she went back to her real life.
<v Ira Glass>这就是斯蒂芬妮在《吉屋出租》队伍里所做的。她让每个人都在情感上投入，让他们关心某件事。然后，她回到了她的现实生活中。

00:56:20.810 --> 00:56:41.810
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by me and Blue Chevigny. With Alex Blumberg, Susan Burton, and Julie Snyder. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, Alix Spiegel, Nancy Updike, and conciliary Sarah Vowell. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Eric Halvorsten. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Musical help today from one of our listeners who sent us an instrumental CD, Rand Coriega.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们的节目今天由我和布鲁·切维尼制作。还有亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格、苏珊·伯顿和朱莉·斯奈德。特约编辑：保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔、南希·厄普代克和顾问萨拉·沃威尔。制作帮助：托德·巴赫曼和埃里克·哈尔沃斯滕。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。今天的音乐帮助来自我们的一位听众，他寄给我们一张乐器CD，兰德·科里加。

00:56:41.810 --> 00:56:42.410
